{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And this shall be the priests' due</b>. This is a newly stated commandment; it was not mentioned in the Torah, for in the wilderness they did not slaughter ox and sheep, only shelamim offerings, so He did not mention it to them, since it does not apply to consecrated animals. But now, when they were about to enter the land, He renewed it for them. This is the meaning of <i>from those who slaughter a sacrifice</i> (מֵאֵת זֹבְחֵי הַזֶּבַח), that is: when they slaughter the sacrifice that I have permitted to you (Deuteronomy 12:21), <i>and you shall slaughter from your herd and from your flock as I have commanded you, and you shall eat within your gates</i>. Now, He did not give it to Aaron in the passage <i>And HaShem said to Aaron</i> (Numbers 18:8), even though He gave him there the terumot and the first fruits, which apply only in the land. The reason is that there He mentioned everything that He gave him in the sacred domain, for even the herem is sacred, but the foreleg and the cheeks and the crop and the first of the fleece are entirely profane — there is nothing in them except the commandment of the gift. And likewise He did not mention there the robbery of the convert. And according to the midrash of our Sages, all of them are alluded to there: <i>the first of them</i> (רֵאשִׁיתָם) — this is the first of the fleece; <i>that they give</i> (אֲשֶׁר יִתְּנוּ) — this is the foreleg and the cheeks and the crop; <i>to HaShem</i> (לַה') — this is challah; in Sifrei (Korach 117). If so, this too is an explained commandment. And based on the midrash that they said (Hullin 134a): the foreleg, in place of <i>and he took a spear in his hand</i> (Numbers 25:7); the cheeks, in place of prayer, as it is stated (Psalms 106:30), <i>And Phinehas stood and prayed</i>, etc.; the crop, in place of <i>the woman into her belly</i> (Numbers 25:8) — they were alluded to there on account of the merit that was destined to be, and he merited for the entire tribe through the merit of Phinehas just as he merited for Phinehas to be a priest with them. And the Rav said in the Guide of the Perplexed (III:39) that the cheeks, because they are the first part of the body, and the foreleg, because it is the first of the limbs that branch out in the body, and the crop, the first of the entrails — for the first part in all of them is given to the ministers of the Most High for His glory."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The Rav cited is Maimonides; the reference is to Guide of the Perplexed III:39."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:32.163137833Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}